# Authentication & security

- Access tokens are JWTs signed via `jose` (`jose-auth-token.service.ts`),
  passwords hashed via `bcrypt` (`bcrypt-password-hasher.ts`) — both are
  Infrastructure adapters behind Application ports; never call `jose`/`bcrypt`
  directly from Presentation or Frontend.
- Tokens travel as an httpOnly `access_token` cookie set by the Next.js route
  handlers (`src/app/api/auth/*`), which proxy to the API. **Never** store
  tokens in `localStorage` or expose them to client-side JS.
- API accepts the token via `Authorization: Bearer <token>` OR the cookie —
  see `presentation/lib/extract-access-token.ts`.
- All protected routes go through `createAuthMiddleware` (sets
  `c.get("userId")`) — never re-implement token parsing in a route handler.
- Every use case that touches user-owned data must scope queries by `userId`
  server-side; never trust a client-supplied `userId`.
- Rate-limit auth endpoints (`presentation/middleware/rate-limiter.ts`) —
  extend it, don't bypass it, when adding new public auth endpoints.
